 ## Subtopics
 
+### [Parallel Sphere Carving Algorithm](4165-parallel-sphere-carving-algorithm.md)
+The Parallel Sphere Carving Algorithm is a method for automatically computing tight bounding volumes for objects defined by a conservative signed distance field (SDF). It solves the problem of accelerating implicit surface queries (such as sphere tracing or polygonization) by generating a lightweight bounding proxy function $f_e$ that avoids expensive SDF evaluations outside the object. Unlike sphere tracing, which discards empty segments along a ray to find an intersection, Sphere Carving discards empty volumes (spheres) in space to find a bounding volume of the surface. It is agnostic of the function representation, requiring only a conservative SDF query, and provides strict bounding guarantees that neural or interval-arithmetic methods may lack.
+
 ### Overstepping
-A technique where the ray marches a small distance further outside the Minimum Distance Sphere (MDS) edge to decrease the number of marches, increasing performance [S6, unofficial]. It calculates the over-stepping distance as $o(p) = (1+\sigma)\frac{|f(p)|}{\lambda}$, where $\sigma \in [0,1]$ represents the overstepping factor (typically $\approx 1.2$) and $\lambda$ is the global Lipschitz bound [S2]. The algorithm marches to the edge of the MDS, then further outside. It compares the original MDS with the new MDS; if they overlap, it continues marching further. If they do not overlap, it steps back to the edge of the first MDS and continues normally [S6, unofficial]. This is not directly compatible with local Lipschitz bound approaches as it relies on a global bound $\lambda$ [S2].
-
-### [Parallel Sphere Carving Algorithm](4165-parallel-sphere-carving-algorithm.md)
-A GPU-friendly implementation of the Sphere Carving method for extracting surfaces from conservative signed distance fields (SDFs). It solves the problem of efficiently generating a set of empty spheres that bound a surface defined by an SDF, specifically optimizing for parallel execution on graphics hardware. Unlike sequential sphere carving, which processes spheres one by one, this method uses four distinct parallel kernels to gather intersecting pairs, compute intersection triplets, filter interior points, and augment the sphere set simultaneously.
+A technique where you march a small distance further outside the Minimum Distance Sphere (MDS) edge to decrease the number of times marched, increasing performance [S6, unofficial]. March to the edge of the MDS, then march a small distance further outside. Compare the original MDS with the new MDS; if they overlap, continue marching further. If the two MDSs do not overlap, step back to the edge of the first MDS and continue normally [S6, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time Complexity:** Reduces the number of steps $k$ needed to reach convergence $\epsilon$ compared to conventional sphere tracing ($\alpha=1.0$) [S1]. For $d=1.0$, $\epsilon=5 \times 10^{-5}$, and $\theta=10$ degrees, steps decrease from 52 to 33 with $\alpha=1.5$ [S1]. Using $\sigma \approx 1.2$ in overstepping variants reduces field function queries by a factor of 1.2 [S2].
-
-**Memory Complexity:** No additional memory beyond standard ray marching state [S1].
-
-**Performance:**
-*   On 432x756 resolution using ANGLE/Metal backend, worst-angle performance cost increases from 0.105 ms/frame to 0.165 ms/frame [S3, unofficial].
-*   Coverage improvement for high conditioning ratios (e.g., 60:1) increases from 0.00% to 12.42% [S3, unofficial].
-*   For conditioning ratio 120:1, coverage improves from 0.00% to 12.78% [S3, unofficial].
-
-**Strengths:**
-*   Accelerates convergence for rays grazing surfaces [S1].
-*   Allows sampling behind the surface (SDF < 0), which is crucial for supervision during neural network training [S1].
-*   Prevents valid geometry from vanishing at grazing angles under high non-uniform scaling ratios when used with conservative fields [S3, unofficial].
-
-**Limitations:**
-*   Can cause artifacts (small holes) in super thin geometry if a single step trespasses two surfaces [S1].
-*   Requires a conservative SDF (never overstates distance) to be sound [S3, unofficial].
-*   Not directly compatible with local Lipschitz bound approaches due to reliance on global bounds in some variants [S2].
